\description{
Density (PMF), distribution function, quantile function and random
generation for the generalised Poisson-inverse Gaussian (GPIG) distribution
of Zhu and Joe (2009), a three-parameter count family with
probability generating function
\eqn{G(s) = \exp\{b[(1-c)^a - (1-cs)^a]\}}. It nests the Poisson
(\eqn{a=1}, mean \eqn{bc}), the Poisson-inverse Gaussian (\eqn{a=1/2}) and
the discrete-stable (\eqn{c=1}) distributions. The general PMF is evaluated
by the paper's forward recursion.
A power-law PMF, proportional asymptotically to \eqn{y^{-1-a}}, occurs only
at \eqn{c=1, 0<a<1}; \eqn{c<1} exponentially tempers this tail.
The \eqn{a=1} case is Poisson, not power-law.
}
\details{
Both tails are computed from sums of the PMF taken in the direction that
  keeps full relative accuracy (the upper tail from the survivor itself, summed until
  the geometric decay \eqn{c^k} bounds the remainder), so \code{lower.tail = FALSE}
  and \code{log.p = TRUE} stay accurate far into the tail. When \code{c} is so close
  to 1 that the survivor cannot be resolved within the summation limit, it is taken
  from \eqn{1 - F} while that is accurate and is \code{NaN} with a warning beyond.
  Quantiles invert the distribution, not a table of rounded lower-tail probabilities:
  near one, a reported lower CDF can round above the actual jump. The quantile
  endpoints are 0 and \code{Inf} (both zero for the point mass at zero when
  \code{c = 0}). Invalid input follows \code{\link[stats]{dpois}}: an \code{NA}
  parameter gives \code{NA}; an invalid one gives \code{NaN} with a warning, except
  that an \code{NA} or \code{NaN} \code{x}, \code{q} or \code{p} is returned silently;
  \code{x} that is negative or infinite has mass zero, and a non-integer \code{x} has
  mass zero with a warning; probabilities outside \eqn{[0, 1]} give \code{NaN} with
  a warning.

\code{GPIG()} builds a \code{gamlss.family} object for fitting the
generalised Poisson-inverse Gaussian distribution with \code{gamlss()}, in
the mean parameterisation \code{mu} = mean (log link), \code{sigma} = tail
exponent \eqn{a\in(0,1)} (logit link) and \code{nu} = down-weighting
\eqn{c\in(0,1)} (logit link). \code{dGPIG}, \code{pGPIG}, \code{qGPIG} and
\code{rGPIG} are the corresponding density, distribution, quantile and
simulation functions in the same parameterisation.
Scores differentiate the exact probability-generating function, including
at small means and near-unit down-weighting. Curvature uses the negative
outer product of scores.

The starting values of \code{GPIG()} and \code{ZIGPIG()} are the maximum likelihood
estimates of the intercept-only model (from the fixed values \code{mean(y)},
\code{0.5}, \code{0.7} the RS algorithm can drift to \eqn{a \to 0} on heavy-tailed
data). They maximise the native GPIG likelihood under the default
\code{\link{gpig_control}()} settings, fall back to the fixed values if that fails,
and are computed once per response (also under \code{gamlssML()}); supply
\code{mu.start} etc. to override them.
}
\section{GAMLSS namespace use}{

The families can be passed directly to \code{gamlss::gamlss()}, e.g.
\code{family = egpd::DEGPD1()}, without attaching \pkg{egpd}.
For families defined by \pkg{egpd}, fitted quantile residuals and
\code{gamlss::get.rqres()} use log CDFs and, near one, direct log survivors.
Counts randomize uniformly between the two jump endpoints; mixed families
randomize only at the zero atom. Interpolation and normal quantiles use
log probabilities, without clipping small tails. Precision is limited by
the family's log tails; a true zero tail probability gives an infinite
normal quantile.
Some separate GAMLSS helpers, including \code{gamlssML()},
\code{devianceIncr()}, \code{predictAll()} at new data and the likelihood
used by \code{vcov()}, look up the family or density by bare name instead
of using the family callbacks.
Attaching \pkg{egpd} can resolve those lookups but does not correct a
GAMLSS version that reconstructs fixed parameters with default links in
\code{vcov()}. The \pkg{egpd} \code{\link{predictive_coverage}} method
instead computes its own held-out conditional information. It does not
change upstream GAMLSS helpers, the re-exported \pkg{gamlss.dist} families
\code{PIG}/\code{ZIPIG}, or residual expressions in previously saved fits.
}

